<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The only exception to the hardened runtime bay asks for, and the one it measurably needs:
       V8 (the app, its helpers, the MCP proxy under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE) and the Agent SDK's
       `claude` (JavaScriptCore) both generate code into MAP_JIT pages.

       electron-builder signs the app with this file and every other Mach-O in the bundle —
       helpers, frameworks, and whatever sits in app.asar.unpacked (ffmpeg, ffprobe, claude) —
       with the same file, as `entitlementsInherit`.

       Measured with a Developer ID build signed with this file alone (script/release-signed
       verifies it on every release): the app launches, WebGL2 compiles and draws, WebGPU gives
       an adapter, the .brf worker compiles and runs a module from app.asar, the MCP proxy runs,
       ffmpeg encodes, and the re-signed `claude` answers a real prompt.

       What was here before, and why it went:
       - allow-unsigned-executable-memory: nothing above needed it. It is the older, broader
         form of allow-jit (writable+executable memory without MAP_JIT).
       - disable-library-validation: its comment said ffmpeg needed it. Library validation
         applies to libraries loaded INTO a process, not to executables it spawns, and ffmpeg
         links only system libraries (otool -L). Nothing bay ships loads a foreign dylib.
       - allow-dyld-environment-variables: nothing sets DYLD_*.
       - files.user-selected.read-write: an App Sandbox entitlement; bay is not sandboxed, so it
         granted nothing.
       Add one back only with the failure it fixes, measured in a signed build. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
